Context: Grothendieck is setting up the stratification of the Deligne–Mumford–Knudsen compactification of M_{g,ν} by combinatorial type, the 'structure at infinity'. A 'maquette', or marked MD-graph, G is the combinatorial type of a stable marked curve. It consists of:
- a finite set S of vertices with a genus function g: S → N;
- a finite set Ã of half-edges (branches at the nodes), with a fixed-point-free involution σ pairing the two branches of each node;
- a set I of marked points;
- incidence maps Ã → S and I → S.
On the previous page he defined a stable curve over an algebraically closed field to be of type G if its dual marked graph is isomorphic to G. A G-épinglage (G-pinning) is the choice of such an isomorphism.

This page relativizes to an arbitrary base 𝕊. For a stable curve (X̂, I) over 𝕊 of type G, the dual graphs of the geometric fibres are claimed to form a 'scheme in maquettes' G(X̂, I) over 𝕊. This is a system of finite étale 𝕊-schemes and morphisms (S, Ã, σ_Ã, I, Ã → S, I → S, together with S → N_𝕊), étale-locally isomorphic to the constant maquette G_𝕊. In modern terms, the dual graph is a locally constant étale sheaf of graphs on the stratum, i.e. a monodromy-twisted form of G.

A G-pinning of (X̂, I) is then an isomorphism G_𝕊 ≅ G(X̂, I). With Γ = Aut G finite (18), G-pinnings are the sections of a Γ_𝕊-torsor, the torsor of G-pinnings. This is the standard fact that twisted forms of G are classified by H^1(𝕊, Aut G).

He then fixes 𝕊 and considers the groupoid of 'standard' G-pinned curves. For each vertex α he sets Î_α = I_α ⊔ Ã_α, the marked points plus branches at α, with cardinality ν̂_α, the total weight or valence of α. The next page completes the construction:
- a standard G-pinned curve is equivalent to giving, for each α, a smooth proper curve Y_α of genus g_α with an Î_α-pointing;
- hence M_G(𝕊) ≃ ∏_α M_{g_α, Î_α}(𝕊) (21).
- The open stratum of type G is then the quotient of this product by Γ = Aut G via the torsor.

This is the now-standard description of the boundary strata of M̄_{g,n} as [∏_v M_{g_v,n(v)} / Aut G], which underlies the clutching/gluing maps and the modular-operad structure.
